   }
  ],
  "notes": "upadhā is kept untranslated (glossary, 13.4); here it very probably refers to the tests of ministers, but the line itself does not say so. The instrumental upadhābhiḥ is taken with śuddha (\"pure through the upadhās\"); the alternative is to take it with utpādayet (\"should produce by the upadhās\"). udāsthita is kept as a term (explained in unit 4). gṛha-patika, vaidehaka (trader), tāpasa (ascetic) and the vyañjana compound (bahuvrīhi \"having the guise of X\") are explained in the units that follow. gūḍha-puruṣa and sattrin both come out as \"secret agents\" under the glossary; sattrin, tīkṣṇa (assassin), rasada (poisoner) and bhikṣukī (female mendicant) are taken as further agent classes parallel to the vyañjana agents, joined by ca. In Hindi the list closed by ca takes commas only.",
  "confidence": "medium"
 },
